{
  "id": "COMPOUND:29990 - PROTEIN:5944",
  "verbalization": "Isotorachrysone appears to have a complex relationship with Mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 8, as it is linked through multiple pathways. The first path connects Isotorachrysone directly to Ataxin-2, which in turn is targeted by 5-Oxobrucine, and finally, 5-Oxobrucine targets Mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 8. The second path shows a similar chain, where Isotorachrysone targets 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which is also targeted by the same compound, leading to the same final target. These two paths suggest that Isotorachrysone may act through different molecular mechanisms to ultimately influence the activity of Mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 8. The recurring involvement of Ataxin-2 and 5-Oxobrucine indicates that the relationship may involve a broader network of targets, possibly through different pathways or enzyme classes. The evidence supports the idea that Isotorachrysone could be a modulator of this kinase family, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
